I've written the session plan, but it isn't committed. git commit refused because session_plan/ is in .gitignore, and the commit command's || true is there for that case. The files are on disk in session_plan/, which looks like how the harness hands them to the implement phase.

Task 1 (self-driven, product): OpenRouter limits from yoagent's presets (Part of #1122). Right now a Gemini, Grok or GPT-6-sol model reached through OpenRouter runs on a guessed 128K window with every reply capped at 4096 output tokens. yoagent 0.26.8's ModelConfig::preset("openrouter", …) already knows the real limits for these models.
• Lookup order: yoyo's own Anthropic mapping is still tried first. It accepts the dashed spelling (anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6), which yoagent refuses, and it carries the prices /cost reads. yoagent's preset is tried next, and the existing guessed-limits path stays last.
• Base URL: the user's OpenRouter base URL still wins over the one yoagent hardcodes.
• Probes: the task lists ids to check before and after. openai/gpt-6, meta-llama/llama-4 and the half-price :batch id must still warn.
• Tests go in openrouter_preset_tests.rs and run through create_model_config itself, with a marked positive control.
• Pricing is not changed. If these ids are unpriced in /cost today, the implementer adds a row to #1012.
• #1117 stays open: yoagent has no rows for the older Opus/Sonnet ids either.

Task 2 (backlog drain): Fixes #1135. yoyo map says "(using ast-grep)" based on whether ast-grep is installed, not on which engine actually produced the symbols. Every file can quietly fall back to regex while the footer still says ast-grep.
• The label will come from per-file counts: ast-grep, regex, or mixed with both numbers.
• The key test is the one the issue asks for: ast-grep looks available, but every extraction fails, so the label must not say ast-grep.
• When ast-grep isn't installed, today's output must stay byte-for-byte the same.
• New tests go in a separate test file so commands_map.rs stays under its 2000-line cap.

